HostProfile
=================================

DOI: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23313580 (all versions; see CITATION.cff)

Turns raw traffic into one row of 17 features per local host every 5
minutes, so that a model can spot a host whose behavior changes.

A host profile is the behavior of one local host (Site::local_nets) during
one window:

- Input: everything the host originates, whatever the destination: its
  connections, DNS queries and TLS server names (SNI).
- Windows: tumbling, 5 min by default (HostProfile::window_len).
- Per host and window, 17 statistics:
  - volume: connections, bytes out and in;
  - diversity: distinct destinations and ports (HyperLogLog estimates);
  - novelty: share of destinations, domains and SNIs never seen before for
    this host (Bloom filter memory, forgotten after a day of inactivity);
  - structure: share of internal destinations, share of failed connections;
  - rhythm: regularity of the gaps between connections, overall and towards
    the most regular destination (near 0 = beaconing);
  - DNS: queries, distinct domains, NXDOMAIN share;
  - idle time since the host's previous active window.
- Output: one row per active host and window in hostprofile_features.log
  (Zeek TSV or JSON), ready for an anomaly detection model, identical on a
  standalone Zeek and on a cluster.

Columns
-------

  ts                 start of the window
  host               the profiled host
  idle_before        seconds since the end of the host's previous active
                     window; -1 for its first window
  n_conns            connections whose last packet falls in the window
  bytes_out          payload bytes sent / received over those connections
  bytes_in
  n_dst              distinct destination addresses / ports (estimates)
  n_dst_ports
  frac_new_dst       share of connections to a destination never seen
                     before for this host
  frac_internal_dst  share of connections to a local destination
  frac_failed        share of failed attempts (S0, REJ, ...)
  start_cv           regularity of connection starts: std / mean of the
                     gaps (0 = clockwork); -1 under 3 connections
  beacon_cv          lowest start_cv among the destinations contacted at
                     least 4 times; -1 if none
  n_dns              DNS queries sent
  n_domains          distinct query names (estimate)
  frac_new_domain    share of queries for a name never queried before
  frac_nxdomain      share of queries answered NXDOMAIN
  n_sni              TLS client hellos carrying a server name
  frac_new_sni       share of server names never seen before

When a family has no activity in the window, its counts and shares are 0
and its regularity columns -1. Example row (CICIDS-2017, a workstation):

  ts 1499428800.0  host 192.168.10.14  idle_before -1.0  n_conns 204
  bytes_out 787363  bytes_in 941544  n_dst 43  n_dst_ports 18
  frac_new_dst 0.22  frac_internal_dst 0.71  frac_failed 0.02
  start_cv 3.56  beacon_cv 0.69  n_dns 366  n_domains 74
  frac_new_domain 0.21  frac_nxdomain 0.01  n_sni 57  frac_new_sni 0.54

Configuration
-------------

By default Zeek treats every private or reserved range as local, so hosts
of those ranges are profiled too. Declare your own networks explicitly:

  redef Site::private_address_space_is_local = F;
  redef Site::local_nets = { 10.1.0.0/16 };   # your networks

Cluster
-------

Works on any Zeek cluster, Broker or ZeroMQ, with the same output as a
standalone Zeek. Workers send each observation (connection, DNS message,
SNI) to the proxy owning its host (chosen by hashing the host), which keeps
the windows and writes the rows. Without a proxy, the manager does it and
warns at startup: add proxies to spread the load.

Resources
---------

Measured on Zeek 9.0 (one process, CICIDS-2017 Friday capture, 8.3 GB, 28
profiled hosts), two runs each:

  CPU      +1 to +9 % user time over plain Zeek (runs vary by ~10 %)
  memory   +1 to 2 MB peak on that capture

Memory grows with the number of profiled hosts, mostly the novelty memory
(one Bloom filter of about 18 KB per kind: destinations, domains, SNIs; two
after a rotation). Measured per host:

  long-lived state       54 KB, up to 107 KB once filters have rotated
  open window (active)   7 KB

So about 110 MB per 1,000 hosts in the worst case. Hosts are forgotten after
HostProfile::host_expire (1 day) of inactivity. The filter size scales with
HostProfile::novelty_capacity (10,000 keys) and log(1 / novelty_fp): e.g.
novelty_capacity = 2000 divides it by 5, at the cost of forgetting sooner.
In a cluster this memory lives on the proxies, spread across them.

Layout
------

  scripts/main.zeek            windowing engine, cluster dispatch, distinct
                               counters (HLL), novelty memory (rotating
                               Bloom filters)
  scripts/features/conn.zeek   volume, diversity, novelty, failures, rhythm
  scripts/features/dns.zeek    queries, domains, novelty, NXDOMAIN rate
  scripts/features/tls.zeek    SNI count and novelty

A new feature family adds columns with ``redef record Features += {...}``
and its data with ``redef record Observation += {...}``. Its event handlers
build an Observation and pass it to ``HostProfile::send``; a
``hook HostProfile::observe`` handler accumulates it into
WindowState/HostState, and a ``hook HostProfile::finalize`` handler fills
the columns.

Testing
-------

Tests use btest (testing/tests/) and run in a Zeek container, so no local
Zeek is needed:

  make test           # run the tests
  make test-update    # accept new outputs as baselines (review the diff first)
  make traces         # regenerate the synthetic traces

The traces come from testing/Scripts/gen-traces.py, which documents each
scenario and its expected values. The cluster tests replay every trace
through a local cluster and compare with standalone output
(testing/Scripts/cluster-vs-standalone).
